  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1237 PM MDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nThe potential for showers and thunderstorms will remain anchored\nover the central mountains through Saturday. There MIGHT be a\nfew shower or storms elsewhere on a given day, but the chance\nright now is well under 10%. The best chance for appreciable\nrain across the central mountains will later tonight and again\ntomorrow night. For tonight, the potential is up to 0.40\" across\nthe Frank Church and Stanley Basin including the Wapiti burn\nscar. There is a 20-50% chance for over 0.50\" tonight. For\ntomorrow night. Up to 0.33\" is possible over the same area, with\na a 5-15% chance for exceeding 0.50\". Otherwise, the potential\nfor more than 0.10-0.15\" in a given 12 hour period is low. We\nwill see a few thunderstorms, even during the overnight hours,\nwhich is likely pushing the models toward some of the higher\nrainfall amounts. Across the rest of eastern Idaho, expect\nbreezy conditions at times through Sunday morning. Ahead of a\nboundary overnight, look for gusts of 25-35 mph (maybe 40 mph in\nsome spots) from the south and southwest. Similar speeds are\nlikely. Temperatures will rebound a bit through Sunday, with\nhighs return to lower 80s across the Snake Plain, Magic Valley,\nand toward the Utah border. \n\nThe latest trends in the forecast for Sunday and Monday are\ntoward most of the moisture from Marie not making it into Idaho.\nWe will likely see some of this getting quickly pulled in as the\nclosed low along the coast swings inland, but the bulk of it is\ndirected through Utah and Wyoming. While we continue to see \nsome decent swings in terms of just how much rain may fall, the \ntrend in the past 24 hours has been to hold amounts down a bit \nfrom the highest end of the range of forecasts. For the Snake \nPlain, Magic Valley, and South Hills/Albion Mountains...the WPC-\nbased rainfall is 0.10-0.30\". For the central mountains, \n0.15-0.45\" with locally higher amounts are expected. Across the \neastern and southeast highlands, the latest shows 0.30-0.70\". \nThis is slightly above the \"middle of the road\" forecast from \nthe Blend of Models. The higher end still wants to push totals \nover 1\" across higher elevations, but the trend definitely lower\nthan yesterday in terms of where those amounts could happen. \nLabor Day looks cool thanks to the rain and cloud cover, with \nmost valley locations in the 50s and 60s with some low 70s south\nof the Snake River. The central mountains will see some highest\nelevations temperatures stuck in the 40s. We do dry out briefly\nbefore see more moisture trying to shift north for showers and \nthunderstorms for the latter half of next week.",
